export const ENGINE_VERSION = '0.33.0'; // OVERFLOW SURVIVES AN EMPTY QUEUE + RECONCILIATION ECONOMY LOCKOUT. (1) OVERFLOW (pipeline.ts s3_buildAdvance): production banked from a COMPLETED item no longer evaporates when the queue then sits empty a full turn — only production EARNED while idle-all-turn does (storedProd -= prodToQueue instead of = 0). The reported bug: build something with nothing queued behind it and the overflow/saved production was lost; it now waits, however long, for whatever is queued next. The anti-exploit holds: an idle colony still banks nothing new, so it cannot save up a Star Fortress "for free". Any replay where a colony sat idle holding completion overflow diverges; snapshot-first loading covers older saves. Regression: tests/unit/overflowcarry.test.ts. (2) RECONCILIATION ECONOMY LOCKOUT (commands.ts validateCommand): set_build_queue, buy_production, sell_building, set_jobs, set_research, queue_extra_research and set_tax_rate now REJECT while state.reconcile is set — the scripted stages never read any of them, so a queue edit never built, jobs/research/tax moved numbers nothing reads, and bought production BURNED treasury BC with no build stage to ever complete it (a live-reconciliation money trap via the map quick-build). Replays of live-reconciliation games containing such commands reject at validation, so those logs load snapshot-first. NOT sim-relevant but shipped together: per-seat reconcile doctrine parameters (ui/reconcileBot.ts ReconcileBotParams — every constant of the fleet doctrine tunable per empire, threaded through runReconciliation opts.params; defaults are the shipped doctrine so existing runs are unchanged), host-local muteTurnClocks (protocol/host.ts — async/vs-computer rooms stop arming auto-turn/realtime clocks from the imported save, nothing in the log changes), and the UI round: the colonies Building column becomes ONE build MENU (components/BuildQueueMenu.svelte: whole queue visible, reorder/remove/repeat/build-now, filterable add list — replaces three dropdowns), clicking a planet in the map system panel opens a one-world management popup (components/PlanetDialog.svelte — production, progress, buy, same build menu; script-locked under reconciliation), the map and colonies table size themselves to the MEASURED viewport (banners no longer push the horizontal scrollbar under the sticky footer — the "can't scroll sideways when the banner is up" report), and the commit wash/edge is suppressed in EVERY vs-computer game (bot stand-ins on all other seats, not just enterSoloGame's local bots). --- PRIOR 0.32.0 CORE WORLDS + continue-after-victory + RECONCILIATION + terraformed-ground defense. (A) RECONCILIATION (reconcile.ts, pipeline.ts branch, storage/reconcile.ts, ui/reconcileRun.ts, docs/reconciliation.md): the async board-game merge. GameState.reconcile (optional-additive) carries per-empire recorded scripts (research/fields/ships/colonize/pop/buildings/terraform/marines/spies) harvested by state-diffing each async save's turn boundaries; while present, advanceTurn swaps S1-S5 for the scripted stages (one-shot first-come colonization claims with hold-and-catch-up, ships at the colony nearest their recorded yard, terraform milestones, androids counted with the owner race), finishTurn keeps the WAR systems (invasions, stranded retreat, assimilation, design refresh, victory) and drops the economy systems (upkeep, marine drills, leaders, Antarans, events, council); settle/migration commands are locked out EXCEPT build_outpost (every 5th turn a colonyful empire with no outpost ship gets one at its strongest world — reach insurance — and a scheduled claim evicts a squatting outpost); spies auto-target the strongest met rival, sabotage hits DEFENSIVE structures only, espionage copies PASSIVE techs (armor/drives/fuel) only with >=10 offense advantage or vs democracy; scoring at ReconcileState.endTurn (min submitted-save turn): population elects a leader (winType council). Deterministic and order-independent by construction (seed + scripts derived from the file set; verified by full replay of the produced envelope). (B) TERRAFORMED GROUND (groundTactics.ts CLIMATE_ZONES): swamp/ocean/terran/gaia palettes shift from plains toward forest/marsh/hills — the richer the biosphere, the more defender-friendly cover its invasion maps carry (gaia > terran > mid-tier); rocky worlds keep their ridge/crater cover. Any invasion on a rerolled-terrain world diverges; snapshot-first loading covers older saves. --- PRIOR same-version: CORE WORLDS variant + continue-after-victory (galaxy.ts, types.ts, pipeline.ts, commands.ts, npc.ts, adapter.ts). (1) CORE WORLDS (GameStateSettings.coreWorlds: 'off'|'central'|'random', optional-additive; lobby select): galaxy generation adds playerCount+2 GREEN stars (new StarColor 'green' — an impossible emerald no real star wears; never rolled naturally, COLOR_WEIGHTS unchanged), each guaranteed at least one real body:'planet' world (green PLANET_COUNT_WEIGHTS never roll 0 bodies; a rolled all-rock system patches its first body into a world with fresh size/climate/gravity draws). 'central' rings them around the exact map center at radius min(cx,cy)/3 using the fixed-point ring tables (ROTATIONS + new RING_POINTS 9/10); 'random' scatters them with 450cp mutual spacing relaxing to 300/150 when the map is tight. Core stars are placed FIRST (ordinary stars flow around them), are ADDITIVE to the size's star count, never host homeworlds or wormholes, are never guarded by keepers nor chosen as Orion, and are excluded from advanced-start regions and big-start bubbles (the victory stars start unowned). The designated victory world per green star (biggest, innermost on ties) rides GeneratedGalaxy.coreWorlds -> GameState.coreWorlds (sorted planet ids, optional-additive). s12_victory: the first live empire holding a real (non-outpost, >=1000 popK) colony on EVERY designated world wins with new winType 'core_worlds' (distinct planets have distinct owners, so at most one empire can qualify per turn). Core worlds force the standard generator — mirror is superseded while on (lobby disables the checkbox). (2) CONTINUE AFTER VICTORY: new continue_game command (any live empire, only after a declared win) latches GameState.victoryContinued (optional-additive) — the win stays on record (winner/winType untouched) but adapter.winnerOf returns null so hosts/sessions/bots resume turns; UI gates (autopilot, auto-explore, research queue, fast-forward, hotkeys) and SoloBot/governor honor the flag. Old saves never carry the new fields (cross-version loads are snapshot-first per the compatibility contract), and generation with the variant OFF draws the identical rng stream as 0.31 — core placement only rolls when enabled. bows on the enemy + crossing-motion gunnery (combat.ts, spaceTactics.ts; tactics battles only — non-tactics replays byte-exact). The replay report: ships "keep facing away from the enemy... a bunch of tumbling leaves". Root cause: tacticalAnchor computed each ship's choreographed FACING from its assigned STATION toward the enemy, not from the ship — a ship far from its slot (a rotating envelop ring, a charge weave, a strike dive) was ordered to point wherever its distant anchor happened to look, and the helm chased that moving error every tick. (1) THE BOW IS JUDGED FROM THE DECK (the 0.26.3 capital rule, now for everyone): charge riders, strike-wing dives and every wall/ring station face their live target (else the enemy mass) from where the ship actually IS; staging sprints still face along the run, a running standoff still shows the broadside, a raid still faces its lane. Measured by the new facing probe (tests/balance/facing-probe.test.ts, MOO2_FACING=1): the screenshot-shaped assault (11 v 2 + base, envelop) goes from 17% of ship-ticks bow-on-enemy (36% pointed >135° AWAY) to 98% bow-on, churn 0.63->0.27 steps/tick; every doctrine pair improves. A side effect with teeth: divers now close nose-first at FULL BURN (the old backward facing threw the astern-course speed penalty, so charges crawled in), which made charge degenerate (+15.6 pooled edge, every archetype's best answer) and forced the rest of this entry. (2) CROSSING-MOTION EVASION: motion evasion counts only the component ACROSS the shooter's line of fire — a ship boring straight down the gun line holds a constant bearing and is easy to hit however hot its drives, so a head-on charge eats its approach again (in the gunnery, where it belongs, instead of in a crawl). Orbits, weaves and withdrawals keep earning. EVASION_MAX 30->24 (a knife-range orbit sits ON the cap under the crossing rule where the scalar rule only brushed it); passthrough keeps the scalar rule at the old ceiling (RAID_EVASION_MAX 30) — a raid spends every point of way on evasive running, and "speed is the armor" is its entire offer. (3) STEADY AIM (STEADY_AIM_MAX 10): direct fire from a steady deck is up to 10 to-hit points better, decaying at EVASION_PER_MP per MP the SHOOTER spent translating — the mirror of motion evasion and the wall's answer to run-and-gun (standing still now buys accuracy as well as jukes; the line was structurally sunk by (2) without it). (4) THE WITHDRAWAL ZIGZAGS: a giving-ground fleet tacks +/-2 heading steps every 8 ticks — a dead-stern run has zero crossing motion and was a free kill under (2); a FIGHTING withdrawal trades a little ground for staying alive while it gives it. (5) OVERLAPPING FIELDS OF FIRE: fire-discipline retargeting (0.26.3) now also treats an all-forward-battery ship's locked target as unworkable when it sits beyond juke reach (outside PATTERN_MELEE), and a LINE wall additionally masses its fire on divers that got IN AMONG it (within melee of any wall ship; focus = workable enemy nearest the wall's own mass, a shared deterministic pick) — six private duels against knife-range orbiters lost the wall outright; six steady decks on one diver at a time kill it before it collects, which is what a line of mutually supporting ships IS. At gun range the wall keeps its ordered priority. (6) DOCTRINE_PROFILE retune: charge fireBand 2->1 (it crosses the long band in silence now that it actually crosses at speed), line fireBand 1->2 (the wall works the long band, or a full-burn dive is on top of it before it fires a meaningful shot); and fire discipline never gates direct fire AT a base — a base already fires from any range off a steady deck at closers who now have zero head-on evasion, so an assault that held its reply until medium band lost the approach outright (the battleids siege fixture caught it: three battleships fell to a frigate and a star base). Rebalanced over the full Monte Carlo (tests/balance/space-tactics-sim.test.ts, report regenerated): pooled per-doctrine means within [-11.2, +6.9] vs [-10.4, +5.6] on 0.30, dominance spread 18.1 vs 15.9, all six doctrines still the right answer somewhere, all spacedoctrine/battle-lab fixtures pass unchanged. Sim state adds per-tick displacement tracking (Sim.dxTick/dyTick, battle-local). Any `tactics` battle diverges from a 0.30 re-sim by design; snapshot-first loading covers older saves. --- PRIOR 0.30.0 TAX AT PAR + MOVERS KEEP THEIR JOB + REPEAT BUILD (economy.ts, commands.ts, pipeline.ts, types.ts, items.ts). (1) TAX AT PAR: the empire tax mints 1 BC per taxed production point (economy.ts taxBC = taxedProd; was 2:1, identical to trade goods — the player report: "taxing 1 BC of production should give 1 full BC; trade goods is the 2:1 conversion unless fantastic traders"). Trade goods stays 2 prod -> 1 BC (fantastic traders 1:1): its discount is the price of converting the WHOLE queue on demand, while the tax skims a slice at full value. Any replay with a nonzero tax rate diverges; snapshot-first loading covers older saves. The prod breakdown now shows the skim (−N empire tax) and prod/sci breakdowns end in an unconditional total line, so no output cell ever hovers to an EMPTY tooltip (android-only colonies with no scientists used to). (2) ORGANIC MOVERS KEEP THEIR JOB: move_colonists arrivals land in the job they vacated — fromJob rides PopTransit.job for ORGANICS too now, not just hardwired androids (commands.ts in-system + transit push, pipeline.ts landing unchanged: dst[t.job ?? 'workers']) — a dragged farmer is still a farmer on the other side instead of being dumped into production (the reported bug). Commands that name no fromJob (bots, legacy) still land as workers, and their transits carry no job key, so pre-0.30 logs hash identically; organic transits FROM this version carry the key and diverge by design. (3) REPEAT BUILD: QueueItem gains optional `repeat` (absent = off, so old saves hash unchanged); set_build_queue items accept {item, repeat} objects alongside plain strings; repeat is legal only where building another copy can mean anything — ships and repeatable projects (items.itemMayRepeat), never unique buildings, never housing/trade_goods (they never complete). On completion a repeating head STAYS at the head and builds the next copy (s3_buildAdvance; up to the 10-per-turn guard), retiring itself the moment canQueue refuses another (android compartments full, spy roster full, terraform topped out...). UI: Spreadsheet gets a per-row ⟳ repeat toggle on the active build, a per-row "⤴ now" front-insert (the displaced build keeps slot 1 and its place — no more change-head-then-requeue), queue edits preserve other entries' repeat flags (ColonyRow.queueEntries), and rows highlight under hover/focus so the far-right queue columns stay visually tied to their planet. NOT sim-relevant but shipped together: the report-feed cursor now diffs a monotonic reportsSeq instead of reports.length (the 300-cap froze length and silently killed research celebrations, arrival pings and the unread badge for the rest of the game — the "tech finished and it didn't tell me" report), lastTurnEvents is replaced even when a boundary yields zero events (stale-list phantom duplicates), ship_arrived events now drive a one-boundary fleet-arrival overlay grouped per star, and research applications show their effect text inline on the choice list (data/index.ts itemDescription + curated text for the always-known basics). --- PRIOR 0.29.0 ANDROIDS: HARDWIRED JOBS RESTORED + RELOCATION UNLOCKED (commands.ts, pipeline.ts, types.ts). 0.28.1's rewiring was wrong: an android is hardwired at the factory to the job it was built for, FOR LIFE. (1) set_jobs again only ever RESTATES an android group's split verbatim ("hardwired to the job they were built for") — replays containing a 0.28.1 rewire now reject at validation, so those logs load snapshot-first. (2) NEW: move_colonists accepts androids, near or far, on the same logistics as organics (in-system shuttle free/instant; between systems 5 freighters per unit plus travel time) — but the command must name fromJob (validated against the group's actual count in that job) and the movers remain EXACTLY that job at the destination: in-system arrivals land in fromJob, between-system transits carry the new optional PopTransit.job (key present only on android transits, so organic-only saves hash unchanged) and the landing routes them into it. (3) Android destination room is androidCap (2×sizeClass subterranean compartments) counting android units en route — never the organic climate ceiling — and the organic cap check now counts only ORGANIC transits en route (an inbound android convoy no longer eats organic headroom). Spreadsheet UI: dragging an android onto another colony relocates it; a same-colony job-column drop is refused with a note, and the farmers cell accepts android drops even where nothing grows (android farmers synthesize food). --- PRIOR 0.28.1 (REVERTED above) androids rewire jobs freely WITHIN their colony; move_colonists still refused to let an android leave. --- PRIOR 0.28.0 NO USELESS BUILDS + default designs roll model variants. (1) USELESS-TECH GATES (items.ts canQueue): builds that can never do anything at a colony are no longer offered or accepted — gravity_generator where no organic resident (nor the owner race) suffers a gravity penalty on the planet (economy.colonyGravityPenalized; androids/natives are gravity-neutral), and the morale-only buildings (holo_simulator, virtual_reality_network, pleasure_dome) for unification governments, whose colonists are morale-immune (economy.moralePct returns 0; covers galactic_unification too since resolveTraits keeps government 'unification'). Replays containing such a queue command now reject at validation, so older logs load snapshot-first. (2) DEFAULT DESIGNS ROLL MODEL VARIANTS (shipdesign.rollModelIdx, adapter init + pipeline s11): engine-maintained default designs get a pseudo-random modelIdx from stable ids (deterministic in replays) instead of implicitly wearing whatever their design id happened to wrap to, and every s11 refresh strides the variant by an offset that is nonzero mod 3 AND mod 4 — the art wraps warship models mod 4 (titans mod 3), so an upgraded default hull ALWAYS renders visibly different from the mark it replaces and players recognize new marks on sight. State now carries modelIdx on auto designs, so hashes diverge from 0.27.0. --- PRIOR 0.27.0 FOOD MOVES ON FREIGHTERS OR NOT AT ALL + an honest income number (economy.ts foodLogistics, pipeline.ts s2, selectors.ts empireSummary). (1) RULES: the 1-BC-per-unit "chartered civilian haulers" are GONE — food surplus reaches a deficit colony only within owned-freighter capacity (freighter_fleet builds, 5 hulls each, colonist transits still tie up 5 per unit); an empire with no free freighters cannot ship food at all, however much rots elsewhere, and the uncovered lack starves (pop loss, growth penalty) exactly as before. Freighter upkeep (0.5 BC per hull IN USE, rounded up) is unchanged. Any replay where an empire chartered food diverges; snapshot-first loading covers older saves. (2) ONE ARITHMETIC: the whole food-shipping resolution moved into economy.foodLogistics(), and BOTH the turn pipeline (s2_colonyOutput) and every UI projection (selectors.projectedFoodShortages, empireSummary) call it — the shipping the player is shown is the shipping the pipeline charges. (3) HONEST bcDelta: empireSummary.bcDelta was Σ colony income minus freighter upkeep ONLY, while the pipeline also charged leader salaries (leaders.leaderSalaries — the whole bill, 1 BC per skill point per officer), command-point overage (10 BC/point), and credited megawealth bcFlat and fantastic-traders surplus — so the top bar could read "+8" while the treasury crept +1/turn (the exact Hairulex bug report: Lord Torg's 7-point salary was invisible). bcDelta now sums every term the pipeline will apply, and EmpireSummary gains bcBreakdown {colonyIncome, tradeSurplusBC, megawealth, freighterUpkeep, leaderSalaries, cpOverage} so the UI can show the whole ledger on hover. Display selectors alone would not bump the version; the charter removal does. --- PRIOR 0.26.3 FIX: capitals fight the battle in front of them under 0.26 tactics (combat.ts). Three linked defects left slow hulls pointing at empty sky while the fight streamed past — most visible in the default battle-lab matchup, where the two titans deployed on the same rank, locked each other as "nearest" at tick 0, and then spent the battle crawling along the top field edge, one of them never in a band it was allowed to fire in (the "top ships turn up and only one side ever shoots" bug). (1) FIRE-DISCIPLINE RETARGETING: targets stay sticky (focus fire needs that), but a gunner whose locked target sits beyond the band its doctrine will fire in (DOCTRINE_PROFILE.fireBand) while another enemy stands INSIDE it now switches to the enemy it is allowed to shoot — tactics battles only, direct-fire ships only, and only ONTO an in-band target, so missile fleets and held-fire walls are unchanged until someone is actually in reach. (2) LUMBER ANCHORS ARE THE FLEET'S, NOT THE SHIP'S OWN: a lumbering hull's band station was the ring point on its OWN bearing from the enemy mass, which chases its own wake — any drift rotates the anchor the same way, so two locked capitals pursued each other's stations in a feedback loop that parked both on the field edge. A lumbering capital now takes its side's crescent slot like every other wall ship, creeps to it by the shortest route, and once it is AT or INSIDE its band it STANDS — holds the deck it has and works the bow round (which also spares an enveloped capital from marching through the enemy ring to reach a tidy slot, and stops the endgame two-titan station waltz). Its bow is judged from where the ship actually IS, not from the station it has yet to reach. (3) CRESCENT SLOTS MATCH THE RANK: wall crescents indexed their slots one way for both sides while bearings grow counterclockwise, so side 0's line crossed itself forming up and a top-deployed capital drew the bottom-of-crescent slot a whole field away; slot order now maps to deployment rank per side (crescentOffset). Only `tactics` battles are affected; 0.24/0.25 pattern battles and all legacy replays are byte-exact (verified by the spacedoctrine byte-exactness fixtures). Regressions pinned in tests/unit/spacedoctrine.test.ts (rear-arc fixture re-anchored on a pinned speed-0 victim so it measures the damage table, not choreography phase; sortie fixture now separates dive/pursued/held-range cases) and the battle-lab default matchup asserted decisive in tests/unit/battlelabscenarios.test.ts. --- PRIOR 0.26.2 FIX: the 'passthrough' (raid) STANCE is a raid again under 0.26 tactics (combat.ts movePattern). tacticalDoctrineOf folds passthrough into the charge doctrine, so under `tactics` a passthrough fleet dived to knife range and HELD there — brawling in the middle instead of passing through, the exact opposite of a raid (what the player saw as "ships hold in the middle instead of passing through and retreating"). Now a passthrough ship drives straight across its deployment lane to the far edge, guns firing on whatever bears as it passes, and WARPS OUT the instant it reaches the far side — each ship clearing on its own (no cohesion wait, so nobody parks stationary against the wall and gets farmed). Speed is the armor: crossing at full burn earns motion evasion (EVASION_PER_MP) and exposes only the bow for the moment in range, so most of the fleet survives, trades a few potshots, and a faster raid takes fewer losses; warp-dissipater pinning (noRetreat) still denies the exit, the standing counter to a raid. Only affects `tactics` battles with a passthrough-stance side; the legacy free-mover (non-patterns) keeps its own passthrough steering, and the patterns-without-tactics tier still folds it into charge, so all pre-0.26 replays stay byte-exact. Regression pinned in tests/unit/spacedoctrine.test.ts. --- PRIOR 0.26.1 FIX: slow-turning capitals no longer turn AWAY under a target-relative doctrine (combat.ts tacticalAnchor). A charge (and a flank/pincer strike-wing dive) aims each ship's bow at its target's REAR arc, so the desired facing is chained to the target's LIVE heading; a hull that answers the helm at turn rate 1-2 (battleship/titan/doomstar) cannot follow it, and when the target is another such capital doing the same the two headings enter a mutual limit cycle — both spin in place, forward guns never bear, and the fight stalls at a stalemate with everyone pointing their engines at the enemy (the "big forward-gunned ships turn away and never fire" bug, most visible in the battle lab where the default stance is charge). Now a capital whose station would be target-relative flies the SPIRIT of its doctrine like a lumbering hull — holds its band bow-ON-target and shoots — while nimble hulls (turn rate >= 3: cruiser and down) still weave. Only affects `tactics` battles that field turn-rate<=2 hulls under charge or in a strike wing; line/standoff/envelop and all cruiser-and-lighter fleets are byte-identical to 0.26.0, and non-`tactics` (0.24/0.25 and earlier) replays are untouched (tacticalAnchor is tactics-only). Regression pinned in tests/unit/spacedoctrine.test.ts. --- PRIOR 0.26.0 SPACE-COMBAT DOCTRINES (spaceTactics.ts + combat.ts, round 9). The 0.24 set-piece patterns all converged on the same range band, so the doctrine a player picked barely moved the result (measured: the per-archetype doctrine rows differed by ~0.5 points of material edge). BattleInput gains optional `tactics` (implies `patterns`); battles.ts stamps it on every newly built input, and an ABSENT/false flag runs the 0.24/0.25 pattern sim BYTE-EXACT — every old replay reproduces. Under `tactics`: (1) the script taxonomy (wheel/split/grand_wheel/maneuvers) is gone. Each side flies its OWN doctrine's figure, measured from the ENEMY MASS (or from the ship it is shooting at), so the interaction between two doctrines is physics rather than a lookup — and the whole balance is a TABLE, spaceTactics.DOCTRINE_PROFILE. (2) SIX doctrines, and `formation` now carries any of them (standoff/charge joined line/flank/pincer/envelop; a standoff STANCE no longer folds into line): standoff holds 320u — the long band, where warheads are at full strength and beams at 40% — and is the only doctrine that GIVES GROUND, withdrawing ABEAM at a quarter of its way so the broadside stays on the pursuer; line stands at 186u and, once inside its band, HOLDS THE EXACT SPOT it reached; charge rides its target's REAR QUARTER at 34u, weaving across the stern; flank/pincer fix the enemy at 150u while 36%/48% of the roster stages off their beam and dives on the rear arcs of their CAPITALS (strikePriority); envelop closes a rotating ring from 150u to 70u and PINS a runner — there is nowhere to give ground to a fleet that is all around you. (3) DRIVES AND TURN RATE DECIDE EVERYTHING. An anchor is a place in the world, so a fleet that cannot cover the ground never arrives; hulls rotate toward their choreographed heading at the HULL TURN RATE, so a maneuver doctrine only pays for ships that can get their bows around; a hard course change costs way (dead ahead full, abeam three quarters, astern a third); and LUMBERING hulls (isLumbering) fly the SPIRIT of the doctrine — shortest route to its band, bows on — steering physically, so they arrive late with their bow still coming round. (4) THE JUKE ECONOMY: a ship may swing off course to fire an off-axis mount and swing back, paying JUKE_MP_PER_STEP movement points per 11.25 degrees beyond the mount's true arc, DIVIDED BY the hull turn rate, out of the movement it did not spend going anywhere (JUKE_MAX_OFF caps it at FX coverage; being enveloped doubles the price). The other half of that bargain is MOTION EVASION: direct fire is EVASION_PER_MP to-hit points less likely to land per movement point the target actually spent TRANSLATING (capped at EVASION_MAX; turning on the spot buys nothing; guided munitions are unaffected). So a wall that stands to work its guns fires most of its battery off-axis and is the easiest thing on the field to hit, and a fleet dancing through the medium band is the reverse. (5) REAR_ARC_DMG_PCT: direct fire landing from astern of a target's beam at short or medium range does half again its damage — the payoff a flanking wing exists to earn and a lumbering capital cannot deny. (6) Guided munitions no longer need the bow on the target (guidance is the point of them), which is what lets a missile fleet fight a running battle; STRIKE_CRAFT_TICKS gives fighters and assault shuttles ~170u of reach, which is what makes a carrier a short-range weapon. (7) Fire discipline is per doctrine (DOCTRINE_PROFILE.fireBand): only a standoff works the extreme bands. Bots pick doctrines from their own fleet's mounts and drives (spaceTactics.pickDoctrine, onionBot.pickFormation) instead of flipping a coin. Measured over the archetype x doctrine Monte Carlo (tests/balance/space-tactics-sim.test.ts): mean leverage inside a fixed matchup ~43 points of material edge (was ~24, and per-archetype rows were flat), no doctrine's pooled mean more than ~4.5 points off zero, and all six are the right answer somewhere. Any battle fought on 0.26 carries `tactics` and diverges from a 0.25 re-sim by design.
// Prior: 0.25.0 Ground-tactics retune (groundTactics.ts, round 8): (1) MATCHUP matrix rebalanced so every attack row averages ~1.0 — bounding_overwatch loses its universal dominance, charge/infiltrate get sharper counters (charge 0.75 into fortress, infiltrate 0.75 into a counter-charge, overwatch 1.2 vs that same counter-charge). (2) Terrain-fit coefficients roughly doubled and now cover all 8 attacks, with lava ('l') counted as rough ground for the first time: open worlds (plains/dunes/ice) favor charge/flank, broken rock (ridge/craters/lava) favors bounding_overwatch/pinning, cover (forest/marsh/urban) favors infiltrate. (3) Defense doctrines are terrain-continuous: long_line scales 0.9+0.45*open, counter-charge 0.85+0.35*open, fortress 1.08. (4) NEW groundCompFactors: per-doctrine effectiveness of trained garrison vs civilian militia ("civilians man walls; soldiers maneuver") — fortress 1/1, long_line 1/0.95, defense_in_depth 1.2/0.75, charge 1.35/0.55 — fed into fightGroundRounds' per-round defender power (marines still die first, so maneuver doctrines collapse as the trained core is spent). Both-tactics-absent invasions (all bots today) resolve BYTE-EXACT as 0.24 — the rng draw count and bounds are unchanged on that path — so selfplay/determinism baselines hold; only player-chosen tactics resolve differently across versions. space combat is now a choreographed rock-paper-scissors of doctrines instead of the free-movement sim. BattleInput gains optional `patterns` (combat.ts); battles.ts stamps `patterns: true` on every newly built input (riding next to `slewing` so replays re-sim identically), and an ABSENT/false flag runs the 0.23 sim BYTE-EXACT — every old replay reproduces. Under patterns: (1) each side's DOCTRINE (doctrineOf) is its ordered formation (line/flank/pincer/envelop) or, without one, its stance collapsed to 'line' (hold_range/standoff) or 'charge' (everything else); (2) the doctrine pair picks a SCRIPT (matchupScript): charge×charge = 'wheel' (both fleets close, then circle each other on one shared tightening close-range wheel, tangent headings); charge×formed = 'split' (phase 1 massed rush to PATTERN_SPLIT_TICK 56, then the formed side herds the chargers into TWO pocket circles at PATTERN_POCKET_YS and stands around each at its band — line a medium-range crescent the pockets periodically SURGE against (triangle wave, chargers lunge bow-on at peak), flank a rolled shoulder crescent, pincer arms from above/below, envelop a full short-range ring: every bow bears, envelop punishes charge); envelop×envelop = 'grand_wheel'; anything else = 'maneuvers' (each side flies its own figure: line walls pummel from the long band stepping to medium at tick 160, flank/pincer mains wall a band closer while their fast wings (assignPatternGroups; attacker wings sweep the top, defender the bottom) run the wide corner and pounce the enemy mass from above/below at knife range after tick 80, envelop closes a rotating net from long to short). (3) Ships fire through the unchanged weapon/to-hit/damage/shield code — geometry sets the bands — but arc ELIGIBILITY is choreography-based (patternMountOk): 360 always; FX oblique (±135°); F only when the pattern points the ship AT its target (±45°, stricter than the free-sim ±90 — abeam is not pointing); EXCEPT (a) the fast-forward window (fastForwardWindow): off-axis F/FX mounts still fire in a deterministic duty cycle of clamp(speed,0,14)/16 of ticks — engine power buys forward shots, never a different pattern shape — and (b) with the slewing option ON, off-axis F/FX mounts outside their window fire anyway at a cooldown penalty scaled by hull turn rate (SLEW_FIRE_CD_PCT: rate 4 ×1.2 … rate 1 ×3.0) — slew shots now actually happen. (4) Exceptions: bases and LUMBERING hulls (isLumbering: speed+turnRate <= 6, or <= 7 on a big mostly-forward-armed hull) sit the dance out — they creep at their own drives (turn-rate-limited) and fire from wherever they are — and any ship TARGETING a lumbering hull leaves its figure to orbit the target's REAR arc (±78.75° around dead astern, line doctrine at medium range, others short): its F guns rarely bear. (5) evade_retreat (threshold flips included), the disengage countdown, warp-dissipater pinning, missiles/strike craft/PD, boarding and shield regen keep their logic; passthrough cohesion and 0.23 formation roles are legacy-only (patterns draws NO formation coin, so the rng stream is clean). Bots unchanged (their formation picks map straight onto doctrines). Any battle fought on 0.24 carries patterns and diverges from a 0.23 re-sim by design.
// Ground (0.25, sim-neutral): the invasion round loop moved verbatim into groundTactics.fightGroundRounds (identical rng consumption — landInvasion outcomes byte-exact) so the battle lab can fight REAL hypothetical invasions: pick climate/map-seed/tactic/doctrine/forces, resolve with the planet's one true terrain, and compare tactic multipliers deterministically. GroundBattleDialog round 7: terrain glyphs that stray past their grid squares (ridges ^^, craters o, dunes ~~~, domed-or-tower skylines, trees/reeds/ice cracks/lava seams), tracer fire + impact flashes between engaged battalions, and hold-the-line choreography — defenders keep their doctrine line and yield ground toward the colony only while LOSING the exchange; attackers press assigned contact points in front of/around that line (never idling behind it) and advance as it falls back. Display-only: no sim change.
// Prior: 0.23.0 Space-combat tactics: fleet FORMATIONS + optional hull SLEWING. (1) FORMATIONS: battle_orders may carry formation ('line'|'flank'|'pincer'|'envelop', or null/absent = classic single-mass) — a per-side order that replaces the movement policy of 'charge'/'hold_range' ships with deterministic per-ship ROLES (combat.ts assignFormationRoles; stable ordering by hullIdx/speed with shipId tiebreaks; bases and immobile ships never get roles). line: the heaviest ~half advances only to a wall 168u from its own edge and to weapon range (medium band 224u), standing fast bow-on-target, while the light half skirmishes forward classically; flank: the fastest ~third (floor(n/3)) swings wide to ONE side — a seeded coin drawn from the battle rng (attacker side first; no formation = no draw, so legacy replays are byte-exact) — running to a waypoint 48u from the top/bottom edge at 62% field width before turning in, while the rest hold the line; pincer: the same fast wing alternates ships to BOTH sides; envelop: two fast wings wide plus the remainder as a slow center advancing at half speed (the LOGH surround). Wing ships turn in early when an enemy closes within 150u; evade_retreat (incl. the retreat-threshold flip), standoff, passthrough and warp-dissipater pinning are untouched. DEFAULT_ORDERS carries no formation, so timeout defaults and old logs reproduce today's outcomes. (2) SLEWING game setting (GameSettings/GameStateSettings.slewing, Lobby checkbox, default OFF; carried on BattleInput.slewing — key present only when ON — so replays re-sim identically): when ON, a ship whose forward-arc guns do not bear on its target after the free turn keeps rotating by SPENDING movement — each extra 11.25° heading step costs speed/(2*turnRate) MP (ceil, min 1; effective crippled-adjusted speed), i.e. half a tick's travel buys turnRate extra steps: a frigate (rate 4) whips its nose around nearly free, a titan (rate 1) pays half its move per step; rotation stops once the target is inside the F arc (±90°), triggers only when the stance already wants to face the target (want === bearing — waypoint runs and withdrawals never spin), and ships armed only with 360° mounts never slew. OFF/absent = byte-exact legacy turning. (3) battle_orders also accepts attacker-only invadeTactic (groundTactics.ts ATTACK_TACTICS) riding to landInvasion. Bots: big (>= 8 hull) onion/militarist attackers pick flank/envelop (seeded by battle id), defenders with orbital defenses form a line. Any replayed battle whose orders carry formation, or fought under slewing, diverges.
// Ground-combat tactics + planetary terrain (same 0.23.0): every planet owns ONE fixed 12x8 terrain map (groundTactics.generateTerrain, seeded by planet id + climate; rocky climates carry the ridge/crater cover that favors defenders, urban blocks hug the colony). Invasions now take an attacker tactic (battle_orders invadeTactic: charge/flank/surround/pincer/infiltrate/hammer_and_anvil/pinning/bounding_overwatch) against the colony's standing doctrine (new set_ground_tactic command -> Colony.groundTactic: defense_in_depth/fortress/long_line/charge, null clears): an RPS matchup matrix plus terrain-fit modifiers scale both sides' per-round strengths (Math.round keeps them integral), and the ground_battle replay payload gains terrain/atkTactic/defTactic for the top-down tabletop playback. BOTH tactics absent (old logs, unset colonies) = multipliers 1/1, byte-exact legacy invasions; any invasion where either side chose a tactic diverges.
// Prior: 0.22.0 Bombardment damage caps + deterministic monster clears, both keyed to a new fleet hull-weight ladder (frigate 1 / destroyer 2 / cruiser 4 / battleship 6 / titan 12 / doomstar 24; scouts and civilian hulls weigh 0 — battles.ts HULL_WEIGHT/fleetHullWeight). (1) Post-victory orbital bombardment is now CAPPED PER TURN by the mass of the bombarding fleet at the star: strong (weight >= 12) at most 3 pop units + 1 building, medium (6-11) at most 2 pop + 1 building, small (< 6) at most 1 pop and only occasionally a building (one 25% seeded roll gates whether ANY building may fall; the 60/40 hit rolls still have to pick one). Caps only ever lower the 20-damage-per-hit formula's result — a barrage already under its cap resolves exactly as before, the never-below-1-pop rule and the any-fleet-levels-an-outpost rule are unchanged, and Antaran raid razing (a documented A1 rule, not bombardment) is untouched. Any replayed heavy bombardment diverges. (2) Deterministic monster clears: a fleet massing >= 12 hull weight (12 frigates = 6 destroyers = 3 cruisers = 2 battleships = 1 titan) that attacks an ordinary space-monster lair (faction -2) wins INSTANTLY with zero attacker losses — no combat sim runs; the monsters die through the normal death path (monster_slain to the victor, prize/artifact discovery flows next visit) and a battle_resolved summary with autoCleared: true is emitted to the attacker with no battle_replay (a walkover isn't a show). Applies to every empire, human or bot; the Orion Guardian and the whole Antaran faction (-3) still demand a real battle, and any lair fight below the bar runs the normal sim. Any replay containing a >= 12-weight lair attack diverges at that battle. Both bots gained the matching doctrine: onionBot prices ordinary lairs at the 12-weight bar and sends a minimal heaviest-first detachment; soloBot v2 clears reachable lairs guarding settleable worlds the same way. (3) Battle ENGAGEMENT choice: battle_orders may now carry engagePlanetId — for the attacker a defender colony planet at the star to assault (only THAT colony's defensive structures — star base line, missile base, ground batteries — join the fight, and it alone takes the post-victory bombardment/invasion) or null for a deep-space fleet action (NO colony defenses enter combat, and a deep-space victor neither bombards nor lands marines — blockade only). The defender auto-defends whichever colony is assaulted; its own engagePlanetId is consulted only when the attacker chose deep space: an own-colony planet means 'hold at that colony' and the battle happens under its guns exactly like the classic path (holding never skips or softens a siege), null means meet the fleet in true deep space. An ABSENT field keeps byte-exact legacy semantics (first defender colony's defenses join, classic bombard/invade target picks), so old logs and timeout defaults reproduce today's outcomes; the field is strictly validated (must be a colony of the right empire at the battle star) and rejected by older engines. BattleInput gains display-only planetId (the engaged planet, null in deep space) for the viewer backdrop. Bots: attacker bots assault the weakest-defended colony when they intend conquest and stay in deep space when fleeing; defender bots meet the fleet. Any replayed battle where an order carries the new field diverges. Prior: 0.21.0 debug_spawn_ships optional shipKind: the payload may now carry shipKind ('colony_ship'|'outpost_ship'|'transport'|'scout'|'construction_ship') to spawn plain non-combat hulls instead of the hardcoded warship kind; designId is ignored (may be null) for those kinds, and spawned transports carry no marines (exactly like a yard whose garrison is empty). An ABSENT shipKind reproduces the old designId contract byte-for-byte, so every existing log replays identically; logs containing the new field are rejected by older engines at validation. Powers the mirror-mode bot catch-up (soloBot.ts grants escort + colony-ship top-ups in mirror games with debugCommands on). Prior: 0.20.0 Marine invasions + MOO2-authentic bombardment. (1) Invasions are fought by MARINES, never colonists: barracks train 1 marine per 5 turns up to 4 per barracks building (Colony.marines, optional-additive; older saves default to a full squad per standing barracks), a transport requires a trained 4-marine squad to enter the build queue and launches with it aboard (Ship.marines), invading after a battle happens ONLY via the attacker's new invade battle order (resolved right after bombardment; naked marine convoys at an undefended colony still auto-land in S10), defense = colony marine garrison (dies first) + ceil(pop/2) civilian militia (the old +2-per-barracks militia counters are gone; the +5 strength stays), and a captured colony keeps its conquered civilians while the surviving attacker marines stay as its garrison — troops no longer settle as population. Civilian-loaded transports are pure migration ships. (2) MOO2-authentic orbital bombardment: bombardment damage is no longer bombs-only — every mounted weapon attacks with its STRATEGIC damage (bombs and missiles at full strength with one run per point of ammo, MIRV ×4; beams and torpedoes at half; strike craft never; specials never except the stellar converter; hv/ovr/env damage mods apply). Planetary shields now block their strength from EVERY individual run instead of halving the total (stellar_safety_shield 5, planetary flux/barrier tiers 10/20 pre-wired for when they are un-deferred); sp/emg munitions pierce the planetary shield and ap skips the flat block, mirroring ship-combat semantics. The 20-damage-per-hit, 60/40 pop/building, never-below-1-pop resolution is unchanged. Any replayed battle where a victorious attacker ordered bombardment with non-bomb weapons, or against a stellar-safety-shielded colony, diverges. Prior: 0.19.0 research-target + outpost-raze fixes: (1) completeField now runs BEFORE research.targetApp is cleared — non-creative empires finally receive the application they targeted instead of always the field's first app (any replay where a player set a non-first target diverges at that discovery); (2) a victorious bombard order at a star whose defender holds only outposts destroys one outpost regardless of bomb damage (previously needed >=20 bomb-class damage), preferring the dome on a colonizable (body=planet) world, and bombardment at a mixed star always targets the populated colony, never an outpost. Prior: 0.18.0 campaign-timelapse opt-in: new timelapse_vote command + GameState.timelapseVotes/timelapseReadyTurn (optional-additive) — when every living empire votes, the ready turn latches and the ballot resets so groups can end a session with a full-map history replay. Saves containing a vote diverge on older builds. Prior: 0.17.0 planet-generation overhaul + discovery payouts: sizes skew large (weights 1/4/20/45/30 tiny→huge, size-1 possible again), black holes 6%→1% and brown dwarfs 6%→3% with fuller orbits, body split 62/18/20→72/12/16, gravity now also skews with mineral richness; every monster-guarded system's best world is upgraded to a prize (terran/gaia, rich/ultra-rich, size 4-5, sometimes artifacts/splinter, often heavy-G); ancient_artifacts pay ONE free technology to the first empire that visits the system after any keeper falls (new s6b discovery step, Planet.artifactsLooted); splinter colonies now JOIN the discovering empire outright as a colony of up to 3 farm-only natives (founding fallback grants natives, not owner-race workers); splinter rolls restricted to farmable climates. Every galaxy generated and any save touching these systems diverges. Prior: 0.16.0 colony leaders are system administrators: an assigned colony leader's colony-scope bonuses (farming/labor/science/financial/spiritual/environmentalist/medicine) now apply to EVERY colony the empire owns in the same star system, not just their seat — any save with a colony leader assigned in a multi-colony system diverges. Prior: 0.15.0 android units implemented (cybertechnics): android_farmers/workers/scientists are buildable projects that add hardwired race -2 pop units (+3 category output, 1 prod upkeep, no food/income/morale, own subterranean cap of 2×size, destroyed on capture) — any save where a player researched cybertechnics diverges. Prior: 0.14.0 advanced governments implemented (confederation/imperium/federation/galactic_unification now amplify their base government) and feudal warships cost 2/3 per racepicks.md — feudal build costs and any empire holding an advanced-gov app diverge old replays. Prior: 0.13.0 battlefield enlarged 512x384 -> 768x576: fleets deploy farther apart (the defender line is now relative to the field edge), so closing, flanking and retreating off-field all take meaningfully longer. Combat replays from 0.12.0 diverge at the first battle tick, so older saves load snapshot-first. (0.12.0: engine-maintained default designs per hull class that track research.)

